  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Given that $y=y(t),$ what is the carrying capacity of the following logistic growth model?\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=3600y-9{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=40\n$$\n\nThe carrying capacity is\n{{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nA diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/assets/d8f19dcb707da9aac0ff59b8127778b8b45ef4f55f3fa404b3e589e323c03905.png)\n\nFirst, let's factor the right-hand side of the given equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=9y(400-y),\\,y(0)=40.\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=400.$ Therefore, the carrying capacity is $400.$",
